---
// Location, written as "what is being built around you" rather than as a
// drive-time table.
//
// The subdomain has the drive times and explains the master plan. This page
// takes the one thing a hub can add: the fronds either side of Palm Central
// have their own published completion percentages, so the neighbourhood has
// a number rather than a render.
import VerticalLayout from "../../layouts/VerticalLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@dst/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Faq from "@dst/ui/Faq.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "Where exactly is Palm Central?",
    a: "On the Spine of Palm Jebel Ali, the through-road that connects the island back to the Dubai mainland, between Fronds M and N. The fronds themselves are villa land, which is why the island's apartments are on the Spine and nowhere else.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is being built next door?",
    a: "Villas. Nakheel's inspection of 10 March 2026 recorded <strong>Frond M at 22.10%</strong> and <strong>Frond N at 29.20%</strong>; the six fronds measured ran from Frond P at 20.50% to Frond O at 37.44%.",
  },
  {
    q: "Which airport does it use?",
    a: "Al Maktoum International is the near one — the same airport Dubai is spending the next decade expanding — and Dubai International is the established one further east. An address on Palm Jebel Ali is a bet on the western side of the city, and the airport is the largest single reason it might pay.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is the area finished?",
    a: "No, and the island's own numbers say so: 26.75% overall, with infrastructure at 65.05% and building work at 21.64%. The community a render shows is several years ahead of the community a visit would show.",
  },
];
---
<VerticalLayout
  vertical="palm-central"
  current="/palm-central/location/"
  prose
  title="Where Palm Central sits in Dubai, and what is going up"
  description="Palm Central sits on the Spine of Palm Jebel Ali in Dubai, between two fronds that are 22.10% and 29.20% built. The neighbourhood has published numbers, which is rarer than it sounds."
  image="/covers/pc-location.jpg"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Location"
    title="Where Palm Central sits in Dubai, and what is going up"
    image="/covers/pc-location.jpg"
    imageAlt="Cut-paper illustration: a long horizontal bar crossing a slate-blue ground, with tall pointed triangles in cream, grey-blue and warm apricot standing above it and hanging below it."
    imageKind="generated"
  >
    <p class="lede">
      Buying off-plan usually means taking the neighbourhood on trust. Here
      it does not: Palm Central sits between <strong>Frond M</strong> and
      <strong>Frond N</strong>, and Nakheel has published what each of them
      was — <strong>22.10%</strong> and <strong>29.20%</strong> — on
      <strong>10 March 2026</strong>.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>The Spine is the only place an apartment could go</h2>
    <p>
      Palm Jebel Ali's master plan divides into the Trunk, the Spine, the
      Fronds, the Crown and the Crescents. The fronds are private villa
      plots. The Spine is the road everything else hangs off, and the place
      Nakheel put the retail, the schools and the island's first apartment
      buildings. So Palm Central's address is not a preference among several
      — it is the consequence of wanting a flat on an island otherwise zoned
      for houses.
    </p>

    <h2>The neighbours, with numbers</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Frond</th><th>Built, 10 March 2026</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Frond K</td><td>27.71%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frond L</td><td>24.71%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frond M</td><td>22.10%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frond N</td><td>29.20%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frond O</td><td>37.44%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frond P</td><td>20.50%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A spread of nearly seventeen points across six fronds is the useful detail. It
      says the island is not being finished in a wave from one end but in
      parallel at different rates, which is how a buyer should read any
      promise about what the view will look like on handover day.
    </p>

    <h2>The western bet</h2>
    <p>
      Palm Jebel Ali sits at the Jebel Ali end of Dubai: closer to the port,
      closer to <strong>Al Maktoum International</strong>, further from
      Downtown and Dubai International than the addresses most buyers compare
      it with. That is the trade. The city's long-term plan pushes growth
      west, and an address here is a position on that plan — which is a
      different kind of purchase from one made on a drive time to the Burj
      Khalifa.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout>
    <p>
      Per-frond percentages are a snapshot dated 10 March 2026 and Nakheel
      updates them. Treat any figure on this page as the last published
      reading rather than as today's state of the site, and check the
      developer's own progress page before acting on one.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>
      What the island totals come to is on
      <a href="/palm-central/" title="Palm Central on Palm Jebel Ali, Dubai">the section front</a>;
      what the flats cost is on
      <a href="/palm-central/prices/" title="Palm Central prices in Dubai">the prices page</a>;
      and Dubai's other master-planned communities are covered across
      <a href="/sections/" title="What DST covers in Dubai">the sections</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqItems} />

  <section class="container">
    <p class="source-note">
      Per-frond completion of 10 March 2026 and island totals: Nakheel,
      construction progress for Palm Jebel Ali, read 22 September 2026.
      Master-plan zones and Palm Central's position on the Spine: Nakheel's
      Palm Jebel Ali plan, as carried on
      <a href="https://palmcentral.dst.llc/location/" title="Palm Central location on Palm Jebel Ali">palmcentral.dst.llc</a>.
      Nothing here is investment advice.
    </p>
  </section>
</VerticalLayout>
